WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.476
A forehand lesson for complete beginners, in five parts:

00:00:03.476 --> 00:00:09.560
the grip, the ready position, the loading phase, the hitting phase and basic fixed-point practice.

00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:14.624
The grip: open your hand, point the V between thumb and index finger down, close it,

00:00:14.624 --> 00:00:18.540
and pick the racket up off the ground. That is your forehand grip.

00:00:19.460 --> 00:00:24.000
Note, one: don't hold the middle of the handle, hold it at the bottom.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:27.270
Two: don't clamp your thumb against your index finger.

00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:30.600
Raise the index finger slightly and leave a little gap.

00:00:31.060 --> 00:00:35.780
Ready position: feet one and a half shoulder widths apart, knees slightly bent.

00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:42.300
Lower your weight and lift your heels a little, so your weight rests on the balls of your feet.

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:47.140
Keep your back straight; don't let it sag.

00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:50.338
Arms slightly bent in front of your body, relaxed.

00:00:50.338 --> 00:00:53.560
Don't tuck them against your chest or stretch them out too far.

00:00:53.560 --> 00:00:55.260
Rest your left hand lightly on the racket,

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:57.000 line:5%
Banner: market leader in ball machines · Banner: pioneer in smart sports equipment

00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:57.300
either at the throat or at the handle.

00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:01.439
Loading phase: facing the incoming ball, turn your body to take the racket back,

00:01:01.439 --> 00:01:04.880
step forward with your left foot and point your left shoulder at the ball.

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:12.280
The backswing comes from turning your body, not just from swinging your arm.

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:19.500
How far to turn: your left hand pushes the racket to three o'clock and your left shoulder faces the ball.

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:23.760
Your right hand takes the racket back to six o'clock, with the face always pointing outward.

00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:28.780
Into the hitting phase: we push off with the legs,

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:33.180
turn the waist, drive the shoulder, lead with the elbow, and pass the power on to the racket head.

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:36.800
At the same time your left arm pulls the body round, adding to the power.

00:01:44.030 --> 00:01:47.090
The racket head dropping as the body turns is called the racket drop.

00:01:48.870 --> 00:01:53.810
The racket drop links the power from back to front, so keep it as smooth and natural as you can.

00:01:54.290 --> 00:01:56.990
Don't force it and break the stroke into two parts.

00:01:58.650 --> 00:02:01.830
When you hit, make sure the contact point is diagonally in front of your body.

00:02:04.670 --> 00:02:07.210
Once you have the technical details, we use the machine for

00:02:07.210 --> 00:02:09.090
three easier hitting drills.

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:14.862
Step one, the simplest: start with the racket already back, toss the ball yourself and hit it,

00:02:14.862 --> 00:02:16.890
tossing it straight up at the contact point.

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:21.655
This drill removes everything else that might distract a beginner,

00:02:21.655 --> 00:02:24.650
so you only look for the right feel and the right distance to the ball.

00:02:25.710 --> 00:02:30.810
Step two: we change the ball machine settings to imitate a coach feeding from close by.

00:02:31.250 --> 00:02:35.270
Again, racket back, hit from a fixed spot, watch the ball closely and find the right moment to hit.

00:02:35.910 --> 00:02:37.210
Step three: we move further back,

00:02:37.370 --> 00:02:40.270
and the ball machine imitates a coach feeding from across the net.

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:48.080
Get the fixed-point stroke solid and consistent; later lessons cover the full stroke including footwork.
